# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: local-error-handler-not-called -- an *error* declared local in a defun is never called
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-21

* Symptom

Found while checking the transcripts of =clautolisp/documentation/tutorial.org=,
on clautolisp 2.3.24, under =--strict= and =--autocad=, with =--on-error ignore=
or =quit=:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp --no-init -x '(defun *error* (msg) (princ (strcat "\nGLOBAL: " msg)) (princ)) (/ 1 0)'
GLOBAL: Division by zero in /.                                  ; OK, exit 0
$ clautolisp --no-init -x '(defun divise (a b / *error*)
                             (defun *error* (msg) (princ (strcat "\nLOCAL: " msg)) (princ))
                             (/ a b))
                           (divise 1 0)'
clautolisp: runtime error: DIVISION-BY-ZERO: Division by zero in /.   ; handler NOT called, exit 1
#+end_example

Same with =(setq *error* (lambda (msg) ...))= inside the function.

* Expected

Declaring =*error*= in the locals of a command and defining it inside is THE
standard AutoCAD idiom (=(defun c:cmd (/ *error* ...) (defun *error* ...) ...)=):
AutoCAD calls the =*error*= binding that is current when the error occurs,
before unwinding the dynamic bindings. Programs relying on it lose their
cleanup (restoring sysvars, closing files) under clautolisp.

* Probable cause

The error is handled after the stack has been unwound, so the local binding
of =*error*= has already been undone and only the global value is seen.

* To do

1. Failing test: local =*error*= is called, with the message, and the value
   of other locals of the function visible to it.
2. Fix: look up =*error*= at signal time (before unwinding).
3. Update the =*error*= paragraph of =clautolisp/documentation/tutorial.org=
   (section Errors), which points at this issue.

* Fix (2026-10-22, clautolisp 2.3.28 / alfe 2.3.28) -- DONE

CALL-WITH-AUTOLISP-ERROR-HANDLER (autolisp-runtime api.lisp) used
HANDLER-CASE: the stack was unwound -- the function's dynamic frame popped
-- before *error* was looked up, so only the global binding was ever seen.
It now uses HANDLER-BIND: *error* is resolved and called AT SIGNAL TIME,
with the failing function's locals still bound, then control leaves with
the handler's value. Inner handlers (vl-catch-all-apply, the debugger)
still run first; an error raised by *error* itself still propagates; with
no *error* the condition is declined as before.

Compiled code: a function whose lambda list names *ERROR* gets no
LEXICAL fork (compile-function.lisp), so the local always lives in a
dynamic frame the handler can find.

Tests (builtin-tests.lisp): LOCAL-ERROR-HANDLER-IS-CALLED-WITH-LOCALS-
VISIBLE (local *error* via defun, sees the local A, global back after),
LOCAL-ERROR-HANDLER-SET-BY-SETQ-IS-CALLED, ERROR-HANDLER-DOES-NOT-SHADOW-
VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY. Red against 2.3.27, green now; also run by the
compiled and lexical-locals whole-corpus lanes. Tutorial (en + fr), The
*error* function: the caveat is replaced by a replayed transcript.

Vendor evidence: not measurable through alfe -- alfe evaluates every
request inside vl-catch-all-apply, so the CAD's top-level *error* call
never happens there. The behaviour implemented is the documented AutoCAD
idiom the ticket names ((defun c:cmd (/ *error* ...) (defun *error* ...)
...)), which programs rely on.
